She has spent 10 plus years bringing amazing advancements to AWS and to all our developers to our great delight.

8
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:51,750
Claire is also a contributor to the Amazon builders library.

9
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:53,590
Welcome Clay to Software Engineering Radio.

10
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:59,830
Thanks. Before we dive in, just a bit of a primer on AI agents. You know, you know, how would you define as an agent?

11
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:01,510
You know, what is an agent?

12
00:01:01,510 --> 00:01:03,349
I know it's been going on for a while.

13
00:01:03,349 --> 00:01:06,149
So, where are we today? And as developers, how should we think about it?

14
00:01:06,159 --> 00:01:12,791
I like to define it as three different things. One, a model. Two, tools.

15
00:01:12,791 --> 00:01:15,590
And three, a prompt.

16
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:27,590
And I think pretty much any agent system that you look at, agentic framework, those are the three core things that you need to get started building an agent.

17
00:01:27,590 --> 00:01:36,230
And you know again we all start off with chat GPD or cla the web I guess you know entry what makes an agent different from these typical chat use cases.

18
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:40,310
There's all kinds of use cases you can do with a with agents beyond chat.

19
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:47,350
I actually think we you know for a while there we got very stuck on chat being the only agent use case.

20
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:52,469
But you know I see a lot of customers automating things.

21
00:01:52,469 --> 00:01:57,670
Many many teams at Amazon are automating operations, doing ticket troubleshooting, things like that.

22
00:01:57,670 --> 00:02:02,950
We're seeing customers automating business processes as well as of course chat.

23
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:04,709
Very common today.

24
00:02:04,709 --> 00:02:07,030
Obviously, coding agents are very common today.

25
00:02:07,030 --> 00:02:10,630
But generally I

26
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:18,630
think compared to uh something like an outofthe-box agent like chat GPT building your own agent gives you

27
00:02:18,630 --> 00:02:22,710
a lot more control over what is the prompt, what are the models and what are the tools.

28
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:25,430
You can build tools yourself.

29
00:02:25,430 --> 00:02:28,229
You can pull tools off the shelf with uh MCP servers.

30
00:02:28,229 --> 00:02:33,670
But generally you have a lot more control over what it is you want that agent to do and what success looks like.

31
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:35,190
Fair enough.

32
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:39,430
Now, I guess that's a really good segue into what we're doing here.

33
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:41,431
Strands SDK, I'd love to hear the origin story. How did it come about?

34
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:54,150
When, where, or how? I was working on a product called Amazon Q developer in let's see two two years ago now.

35
00:02:54,150 --> 00:02:58,390
And that was the time around I'm trying to

36
00:02:58,390 --> 00:03:04,070
think back maybe Claude sonnet 2 [laughter] maybe maybe three but

37
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:11,589
but at that time you know the models were not very reliable.

38
00:03:11,589 --> 00:03:22,149
They were great for chatting back and forth, but when you wanted to actually automate something like tool selection, tool execution, coding, they weren't very reliable.

39
00:03:22,149 --> 00:03:34,470
I mean, we started back on Claude 2 where they hadn't actually yet enabled you to put tools into a tool section that was structured.

40
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:40,229
And so you just had to describe your tools in markdown in your system prompt.

41
00:03:40,229 --> 00:03:48,149
And then you would try to get the model to give you the response that tool it wanted to select in a very specific format.

42
00:03:48,149 --> 00:03:51,270
So you could parse it out of this natural language string. But of course it wouldn't do it.

43
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:59,670
So you would try to give it parse the JSON that it was giving you, but it would put, you know, a sentence at the beginning like here's my tool selection.

44
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:03,830
That would screw up your parsing and you would have to give it feedback.

45
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:09,830
And so we started building simple loops to improve reliability of these agents which

46
00:04:09,830 --> 00:04:20,870
looked a lot like trying to parse the the response and then telling the the model what the parsing error was so it can fix itself.

47
00:04:20,870 --> 00:04:29,590
And we saw these wild productivity gains just with those simple techniques of just giving the the model feedback for reliability.

48
00:04:29,590 --> 00:04:34,230
But then we started to models started to get pretty good.

49
00:04:34,230 --> 00:04:36,870
They started to train on model selection.

50
00:04:36,870 --> 00:04:46,150
They started to train on actually modeling tools and tool selection into a separate part of the of the inference request.

51
00:04:46,150 --> 00:04:48,469
We didn't need all of that scaffolding anymore.

52
00:04:48,469 --> 00:04:53,510
And we also started to see that models started to get pretty good at tool selection.

53
00:04:53,520 --> 00:05:01,990
This was somewhere around I would say sonnet 3.5 was where we saw it start to get really good at at model selection.

54
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,990
And by that point we had built up all of this scaffolding.

55
00:05:07,990 --> 00:05:16,310
We had built up prompt pipelines where we tried to put the perfect rag content into the prompt ahead of giving it to the model.

56
00:05:16,310 --> 00:05:25,110
We had done all of this work around, you know, parsing the model's response and we found that as the models were getting better and better,

57
00:05:25,110 --> 00:05:36,710
we were actually making reliability and power of what the agent could do worse because we were kind of artificially constraining the model.

58
00:05:36,710 --> 00:05:43,029
So that was really the the key aha moment behind what turned into strands was what if

59
00:05:43,029 --> 00:05:52,230
we just throw away all of the scaffolding and go towards what we called a model driven approach where you give a simple prompt.

60
00:05:52,230 --> 00:06:00,950
You give the tools and you and you have the model and you let the model choose the tools choose the context that it needs.

61
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:03,670
So as an example,

62
00:06:03,670 --> 00:06:12,469
we stopped doing these these mega prompt pipelines where we would try to query from rag the perfect content that

63
00:06:12,469 --> 00:06:21,110
context that the model would need and instead provided rag as a tool and so it would come up with the query for rag pretty well.

64
00:06:21,110 --> 00:06:23,189
It it knew what it needed to get.

65
00:06:23,189 --> 00:06:32,950
One really specific example was we had an agent at the time that was for uh querying the AWS documentation,

66
00:06:32,950 --> 00:06:39,029
answering questions about AWS and the rag database was about was just the AWS documentation.

67
00:06:39,029 --> 00:06:50,390
But we found out that the agent already knew so much about AWS that the rag content, the kind of proactive rag context was actually confusing the model.

68
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:57,270
there would be an entry that wasn't really all that related and it would just kind of zero in on that

69
00:06:57,270 --> 00:07:04,790
and and it was almost like it was kind of doubting its own its own context that it had in its training data.

70
00:07:04,790 --> 00:07:11,589
And we found that once we got rid of that, sometimes it would query the rag database and sometimes it would just use its own training data.

71
00:07:11,589 --> 00:07:20,070
And so we found that the the quality and the accuracy actually went way up once we stopped trying to proactively give all this.

72
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:24,469
I would love to hear about the origin story of Strand's um SDK.

73
00:07:24,469 --> 00:07:26,310
How did it come about? What was it trying to solve?

74
00:07:26,319 --> 00:07:27,909
What were the pain points?

75
00:07:27,909 --> 00:07:35,589
Well, I started building agents as part of a product called Amazon Q developer three years ago now, I think.

76
00:07:35,589 --> 00:07:45,189
And that was the time of Claude 2 before models had really been trained on this idea of tools.

77
00:07:45,189 --> 00:07:50,150
You could provide a set of tools and you could have the model select a set of tools.

78
00:07:50,150 --> 00:07:57,670
So, we had all of this scaffolding around trying to get models to understand tools and to tell us what tools it wanted to run.

79
00:07:57,670 --> 00:08:05,749
We had all of the scaffolding around describing the tools in JSON inside of the prompt and

80
00:08:05,749 --> 00:08:14,390
then we would try to get it to respond with JSON about what tool it wanted us to choose and we would have to try to parse that.

81
00:08:14,390 --> 00:08:25,110
But the models at the time were very obsessed with natural language and so they would always say here is your JSON or here is the tool I'd like you to run and that would break all of our parsing.

82
00:08:25,110 --> 00:08:29,990
But we found that we started just adding these kind of retry loops around that.

83
00:08:29,990 --> 00:08:36,230
We started parsing the JSON and then giving that JSON parse error back to the model and we found that

84
00:08:36,230 --> 00:08:43,829
the models were actually really adapted to fixing themselves on the next time they would be able to provide the correct JSON.

85
00:08:43,829 --> 00:08:49,910
So I think that was one learning was the models dealt with feedback really well.

86
00:08:49,910 --> 00:08:57,910
The second was around the time that Sonnet 3.5 came out.

87
00:08:57,910 --> 00:09:03,030
This was when models now had structured ways for you to produce to give them the tools and

88
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:09,190
structured ways to tell you what tools it wanted you to run.

89
00:09:09,190 --> 00:09:11,430
And by that time, we had built up so much scaffolding.

90
00:09:11,430 --> 00:09:20,070
We had these massive prompt pipelines that tried to pull from a rag database the exact right context.

91
00:09:20,070 --> 00:09:25,750
And we had all of this logic about what context went into the prompt and where.

92
00:09:25,750 --> 00:09:33,190
And we found that with later models, we were actually making the accuracy and the power of these agents worse.

93
00:09:33,190 --> 00:09:39,430
We weren't taking full advantage of the evolution and the power of the latest models.

94
00:09:39,430 --> 00:09:42,470
And so the big aha moment was let's throw all of this away.

95
00:09:42,470 --> 00:09:45,350
Let's see what the model can do itself.

96
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:47,269
Let's just give it a simple prompt.

97
00:09:47,269 --> 00:09:52,949
Let's give it tools including access to the rag database as a tool.

98
00:09:52,949 --> 00:09:55,910
It can figure out what information it wants to go get.

99
00:09:55,910 --> 00:10:07,509
One example was we had all of the AWS documentation in a rag database and it could choose to go get in new information or it could choose to rely on its training data for the model.

100
00:10:07,509 --> 00:10:18,790
And we found that all of a sudden we were we had these much more powerful agents and they were simpler and they were quicker to build because we didn't have to build all of the scaffolding.

101
00:10:18,790 --> 00:10:23,670
We could just provide prompt tools and choose the model.

102
00:10:23,670 --> 00:10:25,590
And that was the kernel that led to strands agents.

103
00:10:25,590 --> 00:10:34,949
That's that we started building our own agent framework to do that to take on this model driven approach and simplicity approach to building agents.

104
00:10:34,949 --> 00:10:39,990
Probably about a year later, we looked at the success that we'd had internally.

105
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:43,750
We were shipping agents in a matter of weeks.

106
00:10:43,750 --> 00:10:52,710
The QCLI, which is now the Curo CLI, we shipped it in three weeks um using this internal framework.

107
00:10:52,710 --> 00:11:00,790
And so we decided let's release this in open source so that many more people can take advantage of this this approach that

108
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:07,750
enables you to take full power advantage of the full power of the model advantage of the full capabilities of the model.

109
00:11:07,750 --> 00:11:14,870
That was last May I think that we went that we open sourced it and and since then we've had over 25 million downloads.

110
00:11:14,870 --> 00:11:18,310
So it's been great to see the community pop up around it.

111
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:20,150
Nice.

112
00:11:20,150 --> 00:11:29,750
You know, I feel like I have a lot of amnesia because now that you mentioned it, I I can recall the pain of having to describe what JSON looked like in plain English, right?

113
00:11:29,750 --> 00:11:38,550
So, so, so the model would spit out, you know, a closing brace for, you know, matching the open brace and and it would get subtle things wrong like adding back ticks all over the place.

114
00:11:38,550 --> 00:11:46,150
And now we're at a place where you just give it a contextfree grammar and and lens can generate amazingly, you know, syntactically correct outputs.

115
00:11:46,150 --> 00:11:48,630
What a crazy 18 months.

116
00:11:48,630 --> 00:11:52,150
[laughter] You know, it's interesting that you mentioned model driven approach.

117
00:11:52,150 --> 00:11:53,750
Um, as opposed to what?

118
00:11:53,750 --> 00:11:58,870
As opposed to like stepdriven, manual, like, you know, if you were to kind of call this something different, what is it like, you know?

119
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:01,269
Yeah.

120
00:12:01,269 --> 00:12:06,870
I see two approaches that people take to building agents that seem way more complex than they need to be.

121
00:12:06,870 --> 00:12:11,509
One is building a ton of custom orchestration around the model.

122
00:12:11,509 --> 00:12:22,150
And that's what I described around all of the scaffolding that we had built around models that over time the models made all of it unnecessary.

123
00:12:22,150 --> 00:12:33,030
With the model driven approach, you don't really need to change anything about your agent to start taking advantage of a new model and all of its capabilities.

124
00:12:33,030 --> 00:12:43,910
you just have to change the model id equals uh line to start taking advantage because you don't have all of this orchest custom orchestration that's now been made unnecessary.

125
00:12:43,910 --> 00:12:50,150
The other thing that I see people building is workflows.

126
00:12:50,150 --> 00:12:55,190
A lot of folks are building things that they want to add determinism to.

127
00:12:55,190 --> 00:12:59,509
Uh they want to take advantage of these models but they also want to do determinism, right?

128
00:12:59,509 --> 00:13:06,069
And there's a lot of use cases where you do need a lot of determinism, but it would be great to automate with agents.

129
00:13:06,069 --> 00:13:17,110
So things like booking an airline, wanting to automate flight crew assignments to flights, very hard to do, needs a lot of determinism.

130
00:13:17,110 --> 00:13:19,110
There's a lot of rules around it, right?

131
00:13:19,110 --> 00:13:21,269
There's unions and all kinds of things.

132
00:13:21,269 --> 00:13:28,629
financial services companies I see trying to automate things like mortgage processing, loan approvals,

133
00:13:28,629 --> 00:13:36,069
transactions, and all of these things have are pretty risky in terms of, you know, if you don't do the right thing,

134
00:13:36,069 --> 00:13:43,590
if you don't verify someone's income prior to approving a loan, big risk for the company, right?

135
00:13:43,590 --> 00:13:49,991
And so, a lot of folks kind of fall back on what we know as software engineers. We know workflows.

136
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,590
We know procedural code effectively.

137
00:13:53,590 --> 00:13:55,990
So we want to build for loops.

138
00:13:55,990 --> 00:13:58,550
We want to build if statements.

139
00:13:58,550 --> 00:14:02,470
We want to build a while loops.

140
00:14:02,470 --> 00:14:06,790
And I actually find that with agents, it's important to think a little bit differently.

141
00:14:06,790 --> 00:14:14,310
This comes back to some of the additional features that we've been building into Strands since our launch last May.

142
00:14:14,310 --> 00:14:21,829
It's hard to believe that it's almost a year now, but I would say Strand is transitioning much more into an agent harness,

143
00:14:21,829 --> 00:14:31,350
meaning we've been adding the life cycle hooks, the context management that you need in order to build a more reliable agent.

144
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:38,550
Maybe recently we released a feature called steering.

145
00:14:38,550 --> 00:14:40,870
And this is my personal answer, I guess, to workflows.

146
00:14:40,870 --> 00:14:49,829
I think that workflows maybe appear to give you determinism, but in practice they're actually really difficult and there are a lot of scaffolding.

147
00:14:49,829 --> 00:14:55,430
Again, I've seen a lot of workflows developed inside of Amazon that

148
00:14:55,430 --> 00:15:04,310
over time again they just make it less able to take advantage of model improve model capabilities and almost make it worse in some cases.

149
00:15:04,310 --> 00:15:10,470
So we steering takes a different approach where

150
00:15:10,470 --> 00:15:20,069
let's take the example of loan processing for example you want to verify income before actually approving a loan.

151
00:15:20,069 --> 00:15:26,150
The model might often do those things in the right order but sometimes is not going to do those things in the right order.

152
00:15:26,150 --> 00:15:33,910
So as engineers we fall back on our procedural knowledge right we say okay let's create an if statement in there and

153
00:15:33,910 --> 00:15:44,389
let's ensure that the income verification came back with this value if so continue to loan approval but that ends up being very brittle is what we find.

154
00:15:44,389 --> 00:15:50,790
So you might have asked the agent to do loan verific uh loan approval or

155
00:15:50,790 --> 00:15:55,269
you might have asked it just a question about what's the risk of this loan and

156
00:15:55,269 --> 00:16:02,069
so you end up with these very brittle single use case agents with steering instead you

157
00:16:02,069 --> 00:16:09,110
get to define rules about certain restrictions on the agent things like it must call a loan if

158
00:16:09,110 --> 00:16:16,389
it's going to approve a loan it must first check the value the user's income and

159
00:16:16,389 --> 00:16:18,790
it must be above this limit or

160
00:16:18,790 --> 00:16:24,949
else it cannot call this this loan approval tool and

161
00:16:24,949 --> 00:16:32,949
that it kind of flips how we think about software execution on its head where

162
00:16:32,949 --> 00:16:40,230
it's just in time verification of what has already happened as opposed to when I'm building the agent I'm going to say exactly the set of steps that

163
00:16:40,230 --> 00:16:50,470
it needs to take like a workflow We found that it can actually produce better results, better accuracy than workflows can,

164
00:16:50,470 --> 00:16:54,230
which is super exciting, but it's it's a very different mindset than what we're used to with workflows.

165
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:55,829
Interesting.

166
00:16:55,829 --> 00:17:00,629
Well, you know, it's funny you mentioned the steering hooks because looking at the efficacy of it has been is being is pretty pretty impressive.

167
00:17:00,639 --> 00:17:12,150
Right now if you take the traditional agent harness but you know something that's not rulebased but still a harness that calls tools you know invokes the model as life cycle looks where

168
00:17:12,150 --> 00:17:17,510
like just to kind of the baseline where does the agent harness end where

169
00:17:17,510 --> 00:17:21,990
did you find strands was kind of leveling out leveling out at where

170
00:17:21,990 --> 00:17:30,630
you got with the your latest invocation uh latest incarnation of the agent harness one of the biggest changes the differences that

171
00:17:30,630 --> 00:17:42,310
we see and I And I'll focus on the steering because I think overall, you know, we're really working backwards from customers who are trying to automate business processes.

172
00:17:42,310 --> 00:17:47,110
We see that as one of the biggest use case right now for agents.

173
00:17:47,110 --> 00:17:50,150
We have the coding agents like the coding agents are out there.

174
00:17:50,150 --> 00:17:52,710
Those are the biggest harnesses we know of, right?

175
00:17:52,710 --> 00:17:56,710
That we see every day on social media and in our lives, right, as as software engineers.

176
00:17:56,710 --> 00:17:59,510
Those are what we're using.

177
00:17:59,510 --> 00:18:05,270
But we find that most valuable agents out there are probably automating business process.

178
00:18:05,270 --> 00:18:12,630
Often get asked, what is the difference between hooks, which pretty much every agent harness has, and steering?

179
00:18:12,630 --> 00:18:15,430
Very naively, they look the same, right?

180
00:18:15,430 --> 00:18:23,190
I'm going to inject myself into the agent right before a tool calls and and do something.

181
00:18:23,190 --> 00:18:32,070
One of the biggest changes is hooks will only give you context about what is the thing it's about to go do and

182
00:18:32,070 --> 00:18:35,430
you can say this is dangerous or this is not dangerous or I want to allow this or I don't want to allow this.

183
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:40,390
We see this often with coding assistants, right?

184
00:18:40,390 --> 00:18:46,230
You get a hook and you can say, I don't want to allow RM RF star [snorts] and I can block that with a hook, right?

185
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:52,310
Steering gives you the full trajectory of everything the agent has already done.

186
00:18:52,310 --> 00:18:55,029
And as it turns out, for automating a process,

187
00:18:55,029 --> 00:19:05,590
that's super important because you're able to express things like if you're about to go approve that loan, call the loan approval tool.

188
00:19:05,590 --> 00:19:15,669
Then in that steering hook, I need to see if it's already verified income, if it's already called that tool and what the value is that it came back with.

189
00:19:15,669 --> 00:19:22,310
Um, some of the other things that we see is models are sometimes apt to hallucinate inputs.

190
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:31,669
There's a lot of use cases, right, around taking an output from one tool and providing it as an input to another tool.

191
00:19:31,669 --> 00:19:38,150
Let's say you know a user ID maybe you request you retrieve the user ID from one tool you provide it in another bad things happen when

192
00:19:38,150 --> 00:19:47,830
you hallucinate a user ID in the other tool right as input and so with steering you can you can see in that hook when it's about to call that

193
00:19:47,830 --> 00:19:53,350
tool and provide that user ID does this user ID actually match what was outputed another tool so

194
00:19:53,350 --> 00:19:59,830
it's super important for reducing hallucinations as well not every agent use case needs that

195
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:05,110
level of control but I find that there are so

196
00:20:05,110 --> 00:20:14,549
many use cases around automating business process ensuring there's you know low hallucination rate handling data appropriately that

197
00:20:14,549 --> 00:20:20,789
