573
01:01:32,950 --> 01:01:42,231
And then often we see customers investing in AB testing because it's so hard to test agents. It really is.

574
01:01:42,231 --> 01:01:51,270
And sometimes there's just no replacement for testing it with real customers, right, when with these agents.

575
01:01:51,270 --> 01:01:53,750
And so before, you know, even at Amazon,

576
01:01:53,750 --> 01:02:04,950
in AWS, we'd never really invested in AB testing because we would always do just testing [laughter] like we would just have, you know, deterministic tests.

577
01:02:04,950 --> 01:02:10,230
You give this this input to the API, you get this output back, it works, right? But with agents, it's obviously not true.

578
01:02:10,230 --> 01:02:12,870
You're not going to get the same answer twice.

579
01:02:12,870 --> 01:02:20,470
And so we've started investing in AB testing for agents quite a bit internally for our own agents because

580
01:02:20,470 --> 01:02:26,710
it can be very difficult to predict what are people going to ask this thing or

581
01:02:26,710 --> 01:02:30,150
what inputs are are they going to give and

582
01:02:30,150 --> 01:02:40,390
it's very challenging to confidently roll out changes to your tools to your tool descriptions to your system prompt to the model even you know just going 100%.

583
01:02:40,390 --> 01:02:48,470
And so, yeah, we've been doing a lot more AB testing where we roll out to, you know, 10% of users for a couple of weeks and and see how it goes.

584
01:02:48,480 --> 01:02:52,834
It's like changing every part of your car while driving it, right? I mean,

585
01:02:52,843 --> 01:02:53,349
[laughter]

586
01:02:53,359 --> 01:03:00,390
in terms of adoption and and best practices, I think you mentioned, you know, start off small, start off simple, right?

587
01:03:00,390 --> 01:03:09,208
What are some of the common anti-atterns and mistakes you see developers kind of make when they, you know, start off with agents beyond, you know, they build too much

588
01:03:09,218 --> 01:03:11,109
[laughter]

589
01:03:11,119 --> 01:03:13,589
you know, playing with agents is so fun.

590
01:03:13,599 --> 01:03:22,150
And so I can't blame people for wanting to just build a bunch of stuff around agents.

591
01:03:22,150 --> 01:03:27,589
And I think also people build agents when they probably could have built an MCP server, for example.

592
01:03:27,589 --> 01:03:35,190
That is that's one thing that we're seeing a lot internally is uh we went through a year like two years ago where

593
01:03:35,190 --> 01:03:43,750
it was like okay everybody's going to build an agent and then we ended up with a million agents right and there's a lot of overlap between them of course and

594
01:03:43,760 --> 01:03:48,230
it's very hard to understand when to use what agent and

595
01:03:48,230 --> 01:03:51,029
so I think now we're on a path of well

596
01:03:51,029 --> 01:03:59,589
maybe everybody should just provide context to a few central agents everybody's got an agent agent harness in their pocket now

597
01:03:59,589 --> 01:04:03,349
with coding assistance.

598
01:04:03,349 --> 01:04:09,510
Everybody's got um access to to easily build an agent harness with strands.

599
01:04:09,510 --> 01:04:15,990
But often I think the question to ask yourself is should you be building an agent for this or should you provide context to a centralized agent?

600
01:04:16,000 --> 01:04:25,349
That's really the process that we're going through now where we're trying to figure out what is that data that we want to make available to agents.

601
01:04:25,349 --> 01:04:33,270
So uh recently at at AWS we put the AWS MCP server in preview.

602
01:04:33,270 --> 01:04:38,870
What that does is provides uh tools for agents to call AWS APIs and

603
01:04:38,880 --> 01:04:45,109
retrieve skills about how to do things with multiple AWS APIs.

604
01:04:45,109 --> 01:04:52,950
And the the learning there was instead of trying to do this kind of provide an agent for example and

605
01:04:52,950 --> 01:05:00,470
and try to expose it over A2A and all of this probably our users just want an MCP server and have their agent do it themselves.

606
01:05:00,470 --> 01:05:02,789
That's a lot simpler.

607
01:05:02,789 --> 01:05:12,309
That's a current pattern for me is is pushing teams to actually not build an agent sometimes and and instead to think about what are other ways to make your data and

608
01:05:12,309 --> 01:05:24,309
your context available to agents and as they do this what would be like a minimum set of things that they monitor and kind of observe early on at least to know that your agent isn't kind of going off the rails soon than later.

609
01:05:24,319 --> 01:05:38,710
Evaluation is super important for both creating agents and even MCP servers because if you think about the tools that are available through your MCP server, that's going to impact people's agents.

610
01:05:38,710 --> 01:05:44,630
If your tool descriptions aren't good, the agents using that MCP server are not going to be very powerful.

611
01:05:44,630 --> 01:05:51,510
So evaluation is super important and figuring out what is it that you want to evaluate for.

612
01:05:51,510 --> 01:06:01,670
I always tell people if you want to build an agent, start with a use case where you can deterministically evaluate trajectories.

613
01:06:01,670 --> 01:06:05,430
So things like were the right tools called for a particular input?

614
01:06:05,430 --> 01:06:07,349
Were they called in the right order?

615
01:06:07,349 --> 01:06:10,390
Um did they have the right inputs?

616
01:06:10,390 --> 01:06:17,589
The harder things to evaluate are things like chat bots are honestly really difficult to evaluate because

617
01:06:17,589 --> 01:06:25,191
you've got these multi-turn conversations and you end up trying to do these LLM judges around like how good is this conversation? I don't know.

618
01:06:25,191 --> 01:06:28,789
It's kind of up to the user how they felt about it, right?

619
01:06:28,789 --> 01:06:37,270
But a lot of these automation use cases are actually really great lowhanging fruit because they can be evaluated against some kind of existing process.

620
01:06:37,280 --> 01:06:40,309
As we wrap up, what's next for strands?

621
01:06:40,319 --> 01:06:42,309
What's what's what's in your kind of horizon?

622
01:06:42,319 --> 01:06:50,630
We are going farther into supporting folks who are building agents on top of an agent harness.

623
01:06:50,630 --> 01:06:53,829
I think that is really with the power of models today.

624
01:06:53,829 --> 01:06:59,029
There's so much power around that that's been enabled for agent harnesses.

625
01:06:59,029 --> 01:07:05,750
And so I think a lot more around how do you keep agents running for a long time?

626
01:07:05,750 --> 01:07:10,950
What are the things that you need out of your agent harness to do that?

627
01:07:10,950 --> 01:07:14,150
And what are the new patterns for agents?

628
01:07:14,150 --> 01:07:21,430
Things like event driven agents, very very longunning agents that are interruptible are all in my mind right now.

629
01:07:21,430 --> 01:07:24,230
Where can our listeners learn more about strands and get started?

630
01:07:24,240 --> 01:07:26,069
strandsagents.com.

631
01:07:26,079 --> 01:07:29,431
Are you presenting any other communities? Are you talking elsewhere?

632
01:07:29,440 --> 01:07:32,390
Do you have any conference coming up that they can listen to you?

633
01:07:32,400 --> 01:07:36,550
Oh, [laughter] okay. All good.

634
01:07:36,550 --> 01:07:43,190
We will definitely be adding all the resources that you mentioned here in the show notes and in the page itself. Any last words of wisdom for our listeners?

635
01:07:43,200 --> 01:07:47,990
Have fun with agents. Start with model tools prompt.

636
